WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.909
Mind your decisions, I'm Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:03.850 --> 00:00:06.509
What is the value of the following infinite product?

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:09.630
The first term is e over the square root of e.

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:13.830
This is multiplied by the cube root of e over the fourth root of e.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:21.580
This is multiplied by the fifth root of e over the sixth root of e, and this pattern continues infinitely.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:25.120
I thank a viewer in India for suggesting this problem.

00:00:25.200 --> 00:00:31.840
Pause the video if you'd like to give it a try and when you're ready keep watching to learn how to solve this problem

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:48.200
We'll get started by converting the nth roots into exponent form.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:52.939
Next the exponents in the denominators will be negative values.

00:00:53.399 --> 00:00:57.499
As all of these have the same base we can put them all together.

00:00:58.409 --> 00:01:02.359
The result will be e to the power of the alternating harmonic series.

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:05.360
What's the value of this exponent?

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:14.310
Recall that the natural log (ln) of 1 plus x is equal to x minus x squared over 2 plus X cubed over 3, and so on.

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:19.580
This is true for x greater than -1 and x less than or equal to 1.

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:27.739
Substitute x=1 and we get the alternating harmonic series is equal to the natural log of 2.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:30.380
That's the value of the exponent,

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:36.319
so we have e to the power of the natural log of 2 which of course is equal to 2.

00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.540
The answer is "two" good!

00:01:40.460 --> 00:01:48.240
Thanks for making Mind Your Decisions one of the best channels on YouTube. As always, thanks for watching and thanks for your support.

